/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A certification test is a script.
   - e is the distance-specific emission in g/km; m the pollutant mass over the cycle in g; d the cycle distance in km; b the brake-specific emission in g/kWh; W the engine work over the cycle in kWh.
   - A bench reading in ppm is a concentration, a fraction of the gas.
   - The table gives the BS-VI limits for passenger cars, which match Euro 6.
   - Indian cars are certified on the Modified Indian Driving Cycle (MIDC), built on the European NEDC trace: four urban parts of 195 s with speeds up to 50 km/h, then an extra-urban part of 400 s up to 120 km/h, 1180 s and 11.0 km in all.
   - The cycle matters because speed, acceleration and idle time decide how hot the flame and the catalyst become.
   - India went to BS-IV nationwide in April 2017 and skipped BS-V to reach BS-VI in April 2020.
   - Two other rules are often confused with emission limits.
*/
