/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - CNG (compressed natural gas) is mostly methane, 85 to 95 per cent, stored at about 200 bar in steel or composite cylinders.
   - LPG (liquefied petroleum gas) is propane and butane, liquid at a few bar (up to about 8 bar at room temperature), so it stores in a simple tank.
   - n is the moles of CO₂ per mole of fuel (its carbon atoms); M the fuel’s molar mass in g/mol; 44.0 the molar mass of CO₂; LHV in MJ/kg.
   - Ethanol has RON about 109 and LHV about 27 MJ/kg, so a litre carries about two-thirds of the energy of petrol.
   - Biodiesel is made by transesterification: vegetable oil or animal fat reacts with methanol over a catalyst to give fatty acid methyl esters (FAME) and glycerol.
   - Other fuels are used in special cases.
   - Hydrogen has the highest LHV per kg, about 120 MJ/kg, and no carbon, so burning it gives no fuel CO2, CO, HC or soot.
*/
