/* book Worked example 2: Q: A car has a 60 L (water capacity) CNG cylinder filled to 200 bar at 15 °C. Methane’s compressibility factor there is 0.80 (real density 168 kg/m³). Find the methane mass, its energy, and the petrol volume with the same energy (petrol 0.74 kg/L, 44 MJ/kg).
   1. Ideal-gas density: ρ = pM/(RT) = 200 × 10⁵ × 16.04 ÷ (8314 × 288.15) = 133.9 kg/m³.
   2. Real density: 133.9 ÷ 0.80 = 167.4 ≈ 168 kg/m³ (the gas is more compressible than ideal).
   3. Mass: 168 × 0.060 = 10.1 kg.
   4. Energy: 10.1 × 50 = 503 MJ.
   5. Petrol mass with that energy: 503 ÷ 44 = 11.4 kg; volume 11.4 ÷ 0.74 = 15.5 L.
   Answer: The cylinder holds about 10.1 kg of methane, 503 MJ, the energy of only 15.5 L of petrol; this is why CNG cars carry petrol too. */
